Usages:
  convention: .goga/usages/conventions.md
  jinja2: .goga/usages/cooks/jinja2.md

Annotations: |
  The `convention` practice is used for:
  - Working with the codebase
  - Organizing the REPL development cycle
  - Debugging and testing
  - Organizing the test infrastructure
  - Understanding the general principles and rules of development and testing in the project

  This cell is a pure parser of project-level workflow-files. It loads a
  workflow-file (the reading step lives in `load_workflow`; `parse_workflow`
  accepts the rendered text), validates its structure (known top-level keys
  prompt / stages / extend /
  memory / profile / build / assistants / delegation / repeat / gates, field types,
  loop counts, extend-entry positioning, and the inline agent/loop/approve fields of
  an extend-entry), and returns a
  `WorkflowDocument` carrying declarative instructions for the compiler. It performs no I/O beyond the path it receives, performs
  no network or subprocess calls, and has no Imports — it depends only on
  the Python standard library and PyYAML.

  This cell is intentionally DECLARATIVE: it does NOT know about the compiler
  or any compiler-level concept (flow documents, flow stages, the compile
  routine, body embedding, depends_on derivation, command composition,
  loop-expansion, skills merging, stage REMOVAL or depends_on RECONNECTION).
  It returns instructions (per-stage overrides for stages — agent, prompt,
  loop, skills, AND a skip flag that instructs the compiler to DELETE the
  stage; NEW stages for extend — carrying the extracted inline agent/loop);
  the consumer
  (compiler) consumes them. This keeps the import graph one-directional: the
  compiler imports `WorkflowDocument` (and `WorkflowExtendStage`) from this
  cell, never the reverse. The cell extracts inline agent/loop from an
  extend-entry into the model (as it extracts before/after) but does
  not resolve agent names to wrapper paths, does not embed extend-stages,
  does not derive depends_on, does not apply loop-expansion, does not merge
  skills, does not DELETE stages, and does not rewrite depends_on — all of
  that is the compiler's responsibility.

  Use `convention` for code style, dataclass usage, and test layout.

  approve is a declarative auto-approval instruction (one of the values
  "auto"/"plan"/"dialog", validated structurally). This cell extracts it into
  `WorkflowStage` / `WorkflowExtendStage`; the compiler
  consumes it to suppress a stage's interactive flag
  and/or emit auto_approve: true (each value drives a subset of these two
  effects). This cell performs NO approval logic — the import graph stays
  one-directional.

  manual is a declarative manual-launch instruction (strictly bool, validated
  structurally, stages-block only). This cell extracts it into `WorkflowStage`;
  the compiler consumes it to force or cancel the manual launch mode of a
  stage. This cell performs NO manual-launch logic — the import graph stays
  one-directional.

  notes is a declarative note-buttons instruction (a map of note name → prompt
  text, str→str, validated structurally, stages-block only). This cell extracts
  it into `WorkflowStage`; the compiler consumes it to emit the per-stage
  buttons field of the flow-file. Interpretation of the buttons belongs to
  afm — this cell performs NO note-button logic, and the import graph stays
  one-directional.

  trigger is a full stage-body field, not a workflow key. In the stages block
  it is an unknown key; in an extend-entry body it passes through verbatim
  inside the extend-stage body and its value (on_success | manual) is
  validated at compilation. manual is forbidden in an extend-entry: the
  launch mode of a new stage is authored in its body via trigger, not via
  workflow instructions.

  memory is a declarative workflow-memory configuration — an optional top-level
  block plus two per-stage participation instructions. This cell validates the
  block and the instructions structurally: key sets, types, value domains, path
  shapes, and the correspondence between the block method and the stage
  instruction. Materialized defaults live in the document model. This cell
  performs NO memory logic — it composes no paths, decides no block emission,
  and resolves no stage participation; the consumer consumes the extracted
  values. The import graph stays one-directional.

  repeat is a declarative run-cycle tuning block — an optional top-level map
  carrying the optional integer keys max_cycles and keep_runs in the engine's
  vocabulary, verbatim. This cell validates the block structurally: the value
  must be a mapping (a scalar value, including a boolean, is a structural
  error), every key must be max_cycles or keep_runs, every value must be an
  integer (a boolean is not an integer) and not negative. The block carries
  authored values only — this cell materializes no defaults; an empty block
  equals absence. A workflow consisting of the block alone is valid. This cell
  performs NO cycle logic — it counts nothing, decides no stop condition, and
  prunes nothing; the consumer compiles the block and the engine owns every
  cycle semantic. The import graph stays one-directional.

  assistants is a declarative collaboration instruction set — a top-level
  assistants catalog, a top-level delegation block, and a per-stage assistants
  attachment map. Both assistants surfaces are name-keyed maps: the catalog is
  keyed by assistant name and every stage attachment is keyed by assistant
  name. This cell validates the three authoring surfaces structurally (the
  name-keyed entry maps, field types, the required when of every stage
  attachment, unknown keys, the assistant-name grammar, name uniqueness across
  the catalog and every inline declaration, assistants forbidden inside
  extend-entries) and extracts them into the document model with materialized
  defaults (delegate None, gate false, max_tasks 50 whenever the delegation
  block is authored). This cell performs NO collaboration logic — it resolves
  no attachment references, derives no profile ids, maps no gate or engagement
  values, and emits no team or stage-assistant entries; the consumers consume
  the extracted values. The import graph stays one-directional.

  One entry model serves every authoring form: a catalog entry (the
  declaration fields under the assistant's name, no gate/when), a full inline
  declaration on a stage (the catalog field set plus gate/when), and a
  reference (a value carrying only gate/when). An attachment value that
  carries any key beyond gate/when is a full inline declaration; a value
  carrying only gate/when is a
  reference and requires nothing further — the reference resolves against the
  assistant names of the same document at compilation. The assistant name is
  the map key: an inner name key of an entry value is an unknown key, and a
  list value or a string value on either surface is a structural error naming
  the map form. Every stage attachment states when the assistant engages — one
  of start, checkpoints, finish, message; a missing when is a structural
  error. message is the resting engagement: the engine sends no initial
  request, the helper waits for messages and stays attached after each answer
  (gate stays combinable with it — the engine owns the completion semantics).
  gate is
  optional on an attachment and materializes false; gate and when are unknown
  keys of the catalog.

  A full declaration — catalog or inline — MAY carry no prompt and no
  skills: an override-style entry that configures delivery fields
  (agent, env, delegate) and inherits its content from a tool-delivered
  entry of the same name at the overlay merge; the effective-content
  requirement (prompt or skills) is enforced at COMPILATION, not here.

  Assistant names are globally unique across the workflow-file: the
  catalog and every inline declaration form one namespace, and a repeated
  declaration name is a structural error. Every authored name matches
  [A-Za-z0-9._-]; the name "assistant" is rejected — its dashboard handle
  is reserved by the engine.

  The model preserves field presence for the override fields: an
  explicitly authored empty skills list or env map is carried as an
  explicit empty value (an override that clears), never normalized to
  absence; delegate carries None when not authored and the authored
  boolean otherwise. Per-stage env of WorkflowStage keeps its own
  empty-equals-absence rule — the presence discipline applies to the
  assistant fields only.

  This cell additionally owns the contributor-profile zone of the workflow
  path: profile resolution (project-then-home lookup, project wins on the
  same name), strict profile-name validation, activation precedence
  resolution, and whole-file Jinja rendering of workflow-files before
  parsing. The zone stays pure: no environment reads, no subprocess, no
  network; file I/O is limited to the paths received as parameters.
  Rendering is a step before parsing — the rendered text passes the
  standard structural validation unchanged; rendering never bypasses
  validation. The template context is the entire content of the single
  active profile; with no activation the context is empty.

  Use `jinja2` for the rendering engine contract: the fixed environment
  flags, the missing-key semantics, and the strict syntax-error surfacing
  with location.

  gates is a declarative quality-gate instruction set — an optional top-level
  settings block (patience, max_iterations, fresh_correction) plus an optional
  per-stage check-step list in the stages block. This cell validates both
  surfaces structurally (key sets, types, value bounds, step forms, the
  script/agent exclusivity, the required step name, the prompt-to-AI-step
  binding, the script list-of-lines form, the step env string-map form) and
  extracts them into the
  document model. The top-level block carries authored values only — an empty
  block equals absence, and a workflow consisting of the block alone is valid.
  A per-stage value is a list of check steps — an empty list equals absence;
  every step carries a required name and exactly one of script (a shell check)
  and agent (an AI review), and prompt belongs to an AI review step only. A
  script payload is a non-empty string or a non-empty list of non-empty
  strings — a list is the script's lines, joined with newlines into ONE
  multi-line string on the model (shell-identical to authoring the same text
  as a block scalar; the model field stays str). An agent alias is a single
  string — the list form is a structural error. A
  step may additionally carry env — a str→str map valid on BOTH step kinds
  (an empty map equals absence); the pipeline run coordination's gate wrapper
  generation consumes it (the stage-env delivery precedent), NOT the compiler.
  A gates key in an extend-entry is a structural
  error — the checks of a new stage are authored in the stages block by the
  new stage's name. Value grammar is not validated here: timeout durations,
  name text, and env key shapes pass verbatim, and their format errors are
  surfaced downstream.
  This cell performs NO gate logic — it composes no commands, materializes no
  settings defaults, and decides no emission form; the consumers consume the
  extracted values. The import graph stays one-directional.

---

"WorkflowStage(agent: str | None = None, env: dict[str, str] | None = None, prompt: str | None = None, loop: int | None = None, skills: list[str] | None = None, skip: bool = False, approve: str | None = None, manual: bool | None = None, notes: dict[str, str] | None = None, reflect: WorkflowReflect | None = None, memory: bool | None = None, assistants: dict[str, WorkflowAssistant] | None = None, gates: list[WorkflowGateStep] | None = None)":
  location: workflow_stage.py
  annotations: |
    Data model of a single per-stage override instruction in a workflow-file —
    which agent, which environment, which prompt, how many loop iterations,
    which skills to merge,
    whether to SKIP (delete) the stage, an optional auto-approval directive,
    an optional manual-launch instruction, an optional note-buttons
    instruction, and optional memory-participation instructions. Constructed
    by `parse_workflow`
    from one entry of the workflow-file stages map; carried verbatim inside
    `WorkflowDocument`.

    `agent`: agent name consumed by the compiler to compose the per-stage
             command wrapper path, or None when not specified
    `env`: per-stage environment instruction (string map, literal and/or
           rendered). Validated by `parse_workflow` (dict of str→str; an
           empty map equals absence — None). Declarative — extracted here,
           consumed by the pipeline run coordination's wrapper generation,
           NOT the compiler. None when not specified
    `prompt`: per-stage prompt text consumed by the compiler as the stage
              description field, or None when not specified
    `loop`: positive integer (>= 1) instructing the compiler to expand the
            stage into N copies, or None when not specified
    `skills`: list of skill names the compiler merges with the stage's
              pipeline-file skills, or None when not specified
    `skip`: bool flag instructing the compiler to DELETE the stage. False by
            default (key absent / skip: false). True (skip: true) removes the
            stage and transparently reconnects dependents' depends_on
    `approve`: optional auto-approval directive consumed by the compiler.
               Accepted values are "auto", "plan", and "dialog" (validated by
               `parse_workflow`; any other value is a structural error). The
               compiler applies two INDEPENDENT effects, and each value drives
               a subset of them: "auto" drives BOTH (suppress the stage's
               interactive flag when the stage body has communication: true, AND
               emit auto_approve: true when the stage body's roles contain
               planner); "plan" drives ONLY the interactive-suppression effect;
               "dialog" drives ONLY the auto_approve effect. This cell does NOT
               act on `approve` — it is declarative; the compiler applies it.
               None when not specified.
    `manual`: optional manual-launch instruction consumed by the compiler.
              None means the instruction is not given — the stage's own body
              decides the launch mode; True forces the manual launch mode;
              False explicitly cancels the resulting manual state of the stage
              regardless of which side authored it. Accepted file values are
              strictly bool (validated by `parse_workflow`; any other value is
              a structural error). This cell does NOT act on `manual` — the
              compiler applies the force / cancel logic. None when not
              specified.
    `notes`: optional map of note name → prompt text consumed by the compiler
             to emit the per-stage buttons field of the flow-file. None when
             not specified; an empty map equals absence — `parse_workflow`
             normalizes it to None, so the model carries either None or a
             non-empty map. This cell does NOT act on `notes` — it is
             declarative; the compiler emits the buttons.
    `reflect`: optional memory-reflection instruction. Declarative — extracted
             here, consumed by the compiler to emit the stage's reflect field.
             None when not specified.
    `memory`: optional memory-participation instruction. Declarative —
             extracted here, consumed by the compiler to emit the stage's
             memory participation. None when not specified; an explicit false
             equals absence (normalized to None by `parse_workflow` — no
             separate semantics for false).
    `assistants`: the stage's assistant attachments — a map of
                  `WorkflowAssistant` keyed by assistant name, in authored
                  order. None when not specified (an empty map equals
                  absence — normalized by `parse_workflow`). Declarative —
                  extracted here, consumed by the compiler to emit the
                  stage's assistants entries
    `gates`: the stage's check steps — a list of `WorkflowGateStep` in
             authored order, or None when not specified (an empty list
             equals absence — normalized by `parse_workflow`). Declarative —
             extracted here, consumed by the compiler to emit the stage's
             completion gate

    Build the data model with the standard library dataclasses module
    (NOT pydantic, per `convention`). Use @dataclass(kw_only=True).

    Requirements:
    - Use @dataclass(kw_only=True) (per `convention`)
    - Fields agent/env/prompt/loop/skills/approve/manual/notes/reflect/memory
      default to None; assistants defaults to None; gates defaults to None;
      an empty list equals absence — normalized by `parse_workflow`; `skip`
      defaults to False (NOT None); `manual` defaults
      to None (NOT False) — an absent key and an explicit manual: false are
      DIFFERENT instructions and must stay distinguishable to the compiler
    - Field order is fixed: agent, env, prompt, loop, skills, skip, approve,
      manual, notes, reflect, memory, assistants, gates — matches the
      canonical order of the per-stage keys in the workflow-file
    - An explicit memory: false is normalized to None at parse time
    - `approve` accepts ONLY "auto"/"plan"/"dialog"; `manual` accepts ONLY
      True/False — any other value is rejected by `parse_workflow` as a
      structural error before this dataclass is built

    Constraints:
    - Do not validate loop >= 1 / skills list[str] / skip bool / manual bool /
      notes dict[str, str] / env dict[str, str] here — `parse_workflow`
      enforces these during parsing
    - Do not resolve agent to a wrapper path, merge skills, DELETE the stage,
      or RECONNECT dependents here — all the compiler's job
    - Do not act on `env` here — env never reaches the serialized flow-file
      as a stage field and the compiler does not act on it; the pipeline run
      coordination's wrapper generation consumes it
    - Do not act on `approve` or `manual` here — they are declarative; the
      compiler performs the approval and manual-launch logic when applying
      the workflow
    - Do not act on `notes` here — it is declarative; the compiler emits the
      buttons when applying the workflow
    - Do not act on `reflect` or `memory` here — they are declarative; the
      compiler performs the emission when applying the workflow
    - Do not act on `gates` here — it is declarative; the compiler emits the
      completion gate when applying the workflow
  properties:
    "agent -> str | None": |
      Agent name consumed by the compiler to compose the wrapper path, or None.
    "env -> dict[str, str] | None": |
      Per-stage environment instruction (string map, literal and/or rendered).
      Declarative — extracted here, consumed by the pipeline run
      coordination's wrapper generation, NOT the compiler. None when not
      specified (an empty map equals absence).
    "prompt -> str | None": |
      Per-stage prompt text consumed by the compiler as the stage description.
    "loop -> int | None": |
      Positive iteration count (>= 1) for loop-expansion, or None.
    "skills -> list[str] | None": |
      Skill names merged with the stage's pipeline-file skills, or None.
    "skip -> bool": |
      Flag instructing the compiler to delete the stage and reconnect dependents.
    "approve -> str | None": |
      Optional auto-approval directive (one of "auto"/"plan"/"dialog").
      Declarative — extracted here, consumed by the compiler to suppress
      interactive / emit auto_approve (each value drives a subset of the two
      effects). None when not specified.
    "manual -> bool | None": |
      Optional manual-launch instruction (None = not given, True = force,
      False = cancel). Declarative — extracted here, consumed by the compiler
      to force or cancel the stage's manual launch mode. None when not
      specified.
    "notes -> dict[str, str] | None": |
      Optional note-buttons instruction (map of note name → prompt text).
      Declarative — extracted here, consumed by the compiler to emit the stage's
      buttons field. None when not specified (an empty map equals absence).
    "reflect -> WorkflowReflect | None": |
      Optional memory-reflection instruction (file + access mode). Declarative
      — extracted here, consumed by the compiler to emit the stage's reflect
      field. None when not specified.
    "memory -> bool | None": |
      Optional memory-participation instruction. Declarative — extracted
      here, consumed by the compiler to emit the stage's memory participation.
      None when not specified; an explicit false equals absence.
    "assistants -> dict[str, WorkflowAssistant] | None": |
      The stage's assistant attachments keyed by assistant name, in authored
      order. None when not specified (an empty map equals absence).
    "gates -> list[WorkflowGateStep] | None": |
      The stage's check steps in authored order, or None when not specified
      (an empty list equals absence).

"WorkflowReflect(file: str, mode: str = \"rw\")":
  location: workflow_reflect.py
  annotations: |
    Data model of a per-stage memory-reflection instruction — which memory
    file the stage reflects into and with which access mode. Constructed by
    `parse_workflow`; carried inside `WorkflowStage`.

    `file`: the reflection file — a path shape inside the memory root;
            carried verbatim
    `mode`: the access mode — one of "r", "w", "rw"; materialized to "rw"
            when the authoring entry omits it

    Build the data model with the standard library dataclasses module
    (NOT pydantic, per `convention`). Use @dataclass(kw_only=True).

    Requirements:
    - Use @dataclass(kw_only=True) (per `convention`)
    - `file` is required (no default); `mode` defaults to "rw" — a
      materialized value, not an omission
    - Field order is fixed: file, mode

    Constraints:
    - Do not validate the path shape or the mode domain here —
      `parse_workflow` enforces them during parsing
    - Do not resolve `file` against any memory root here — the consumer
      composes paths
  properties:
    "file -> str": |
      The reflection file — a path shape inside the memory root; carried
      verbatim.
    "mode -> str": |
      The access mode — one of "r", "w", "rw"; materialized to "rw" when the
      authoring entry omits it.

"WorkflowExtendStage(before: list[str] | None = None, after: list[str] | None = None, agent: str | None = None, loop: int | None = None, approve: str | None = None, body: dict[str, Any])":
  location: workflow_extend_stage.py
  annotations: |
    Data model of a single extend-entry — a new stage to be embedded into a
    target pipeline, carrying positioning instructions and the verbatim stage
    body. Constructed by `parse_workflow`; carried verbatim inside
    `WorkflowDocument`. The compiler embeds the stage and derives depends_on —
    this cell performs NO embedding and NO depends_on derivation.

    `before`: list of stage names the new stage precedes, or None
    `after`: list of stage names the new stage follows, or None
    `agent`: agent name composed into the new stage's wrapper path (default
             override; a stages-block entry for the same name wins), or None
    `loop`: positive integer (>= 1) for loop-expansion (default override), or None
    `approve`: optional auto-approval directive (one of "auto"/"plan"/"dialog",
               validated by `parse_workflow`), EXTRACTED from the extend-entry
               into the model — exactly like agent/loop. Acts as a DEFAULT
               override; the compiler consumes it against the extend-stage body
               (each value drives a subset of the two effects). None when not
               specified.
    `body`: verbatim copy of the stage body EXCLUDING before, after, agent,
            loop, approve, and depends_on. Open-ended — this cell does not
            know the stage field schema. trigger is a legal full stage-body
            field: it stays in body verbatim, and its value is validated at
            compilation. The entry carries no manual instruction — the launch
            mode of the new stage is authored in its body via trigger.

    Build the data model with the standard library dataclasses module
    (NOT pydantic, per `convention`). Use @dataclass(kw_only=True).

    Requirements:
    - Use @dataclass(kw_only=True) (per `convention`)
    - `before`/`after` default to None; `agent`/`loop`/`approve` default to None;
      `body` is required (no default)
    - Field order is fixed: before, after, agent, loop, approve, body
    - `approve` is EXTRACTED from the extend-entry (excluded from body) — like
      agent/loop; it never leaks into body as a stray stage field
    - `approve` accepts ONLY "auto"/"plan"/"dialog"
    - No manual field on this model — manual is rejected in an extend-entry
      before the model is built; the extend-stage body owns the launch mode

    Constraints:
    - Do not embed the stage or derive depends_on here — the compiler does both
    - Do not act on `approve` here — declarative; the compiler applies it
  properties:
    "before -> list[str] | None": |
      Stage names the new stage precedes, or None.
    "after -> list[str] | None": |
      Stage names the new stage follows, or None.
    "agent -> str | None": |
      Agent name composed into the new stage's wrapper path (default override).
    "loop -> int | None": |
      Positive iteration count for loop-expansion (default override), or None.
    "approve -> str | None": |
      Optional auto-approval directive (one of "auto"/"plan"/"dialog"), extracted
      inline from the extend-entry. Default override; the compiler consumes it
      (each value drives a subset of the two effects). None when absent.
    "body -> dict[str, Any]": |
      Verbatim stage body excluding before, after, agent, loop, approve, and
      depends_on. Open-ended.

"WorkflowMemory(method: str = \"reflect\", path: str | None = None, max_rules: int = 25, commit: bool = False, mode: str | None = None)":
  location: workflow_memory.py
  annotations: |
    Data model of the workflow-memory configuration block — the authoring
    form of the flow-level memory settings. Constructed by `parse_workflow`
    with materialized defaults; carried inside `WorkflowDocument`.

    `method`: the authoring method — "reflect" or "alignment". A goga-side
              selector of the instruction vocabulary; never part of any
              output.
    `path`: authored suffix inside the fixed memory root; None means no
            suffix.
    `max_rules`: the maximum number of memory rules; always >= 1.
    `commit`: whether memory changes are committed.
    `mode`: the project-memory access mode — one of "r", "w", "rw"; exists
            only for the "alignment" method (None for "reflect").

    Build the data model with the standard library dataclasses module
    (NOT pydantic, per `convention`). Use @dataclass(kw_only=True).

    Requirements:
    - Use @dataclass(kw_only=True) (per `convention`)
    - Defaults are materialized values, not omissions: method "reflect",
      max_rules 25, commit False; mode "rw" for the "alignment" method, None
      for "reflect"
    - path carries the authored suffix only — the fixed root prefix is not
      part of this model
    - Field order is fixed: method, path, max_rules, commit, mode

    Constraints:
    - Do not validate keys, types, or value domains here — `parse_workflow`
      enforces them during parsing
    - Do not compose the memory root prefix here — the consumer composes the
      final path
    - Do not act on `method` here — it is declarative; the consumer selects
      the emission form
  properties:
    "method -> str": |
      The authoring method — "reflect" or "alignment". A goga-side selector
      of the instruction vocabulary; never part of any output.
    "path -> str | None": |
      Authored suffix inside the fixed memory root; None means no suffix.
    "max_rules -> int": |
      The maximum number of memory rules; always >= 1.
    "commit -> bool": |
      Whether memory changes are committed.
    "mode -> str | None": |
      The project-memory access mode — one of "r", "w", "rw"; exists only for
      the "alignment" method, None for "reflect".

"WorkflowAssistant(name: str, prompt: str | None = None, skills: list[str] | None = None, agent: str | None = None, delegate: bool | None = None, env: dict[str, str] | None = None, gate: bool = False, when: str = \"checkpoints\")":
  location: workflow_assistant.py
  annotations: |
    Data model of one assistant authoring entry — a named helper
    declaration and, on a stage entry, its attachment parameters.
    Constructed by `parse_workflow` from a catalog entry or a stage
    attachment; carried inside `WorkflowDocument` (the catalog) and
    `WorkflowStage` (the attachments).

    `name`: the assistant name — the map key of the entry on both
             authoring surfaces; matches [A-Za-z0-9._-]; "assistant" is
             rejected; unique across the catalog and every inline
             declaration of one workflow-file
    `prompt`: the assistant's instruction text, or None when not authored
    `skills`: list of the assistant's own skill names, or None when not
              authored — an authored empty list is carried as an
              explicit override that clears, never normalized to absence
    `agent`: agent name the delivery resolves into the assistant's launch
             command, or None — None resolves to the effective global
             pipeline agent
    `delegate`: whether the assistant may create child tasks — None when
                not authored (the value is inheritable: the overlay
                merge lets a delivered base supply it), False when
                explicitly authored off, True when authored on
    `env`: the assistant's environment instruction (string map, literal
           and/or rendered); None when not authored — an authored empty
           map is an explicit override that clears, never normalized
           to absence
    `gate`: whether the assistant's verdict must pass before the stage may
            finish; materialized false (advisory) when not authored
    `when`: when the assistant engages — one of "start", "checkpoints",
            "finish", "message". "message" is the resting engagement: the
            engine sends no initial request, the helper waits for messages
            and stays attached after each answer. Authored on every stage
            attachment (a missing when is a structural error); unknown in
            the catalog. The model default serves the construction paths
            that carry no attachment authoring

    Build the data model with the standard library dataclasses module
    (NOT pydantic, per `convention`). Use @dataclass(kw_only=True).

    Requirements:
    - Use @dataclass(kw_only=True) (per `convention`)
    - `name` is required (no default); every other field defaults to its
      materialized value — except `when`, whose model default serves the
